"All men by nature desire to know. An indication of this is the delight we take in our senses; for even apart from their usefulness they are loved for themselves.": "5a1e3d137148",
"Aristotle classified hundreds of animals.": "2be6a90eee97",
"Aristotle dissected sea creatures on the island of Lesbos.": "abcbc19b9e94",
"Aristotle was born in 384 BCE in Stagira, a small town in northern Greece. His father was a physician at the court of the Macedonian king, which may explain his lifelong interest in living things. At about seventeen he went to Athens and joined Platos Academy, where he stayed for twenty years, first as a student and then as a teacher. When Plato died in 347 BCE and the school passed to Platos nephew, Aristotle left. He spent the next years on the coast of Asia Minor and on the island of Lesbos, and there he did something no philosopher had done on such a scale. He observed, dissected, and described hundreds of animals, especially sea creatures, and many of his observations were not confirmed again until the nineteenth century.": "097e3d5df292",
"Aristotles colleague and successor at the Lyceum. He did for plants what Aristotle did for animals, and is often called the father of botany.": "0e76b0f1b8a3",
"Aristotles great disagreement with Plato was about the Forms. For Plato, beauty itself exists apart from beautiful things. For Aristotle, there is no separate world of Forms. The form of a horse exists in horses, as the structure and capacities that make each one a horse, and we know it by studying many horses. He criticized his teacher openly and with evident reluctance. In the Nicomachean Ethics, in Rosss translation, he notes that the Forms have been introduced by friends of our own, and adds, while both are dear, piety requires us to honour truth above our friends. His ethics followed the same down-to-earth path. The human good, he argued, is activity of soul in accordance with virtue, and it takes a whole life, for one swallow does not make a summer. In the Politics, in Benjamin Jowetts translation, he called the human being by nature a political animal, a creature that flourishes only in a community.": "557af14e20a4",
"Aristotles pupil for about three years, who conquered an empire from Greece to the Indus. His campaigns spread Greek culture across Asia and brought Greek and Indian thinkers into contact.": "5fc95c6e9f58",
"Each field received its own treatise.": "3a7ccceab295",
"Galileo and Newton overturned Aristotles physics.": "1473e027c0dd",
"He criticized Plato with evident reluctance.": "cb574fa222e4",
"He served as tutor to the young Alexander.": "75c1d0cd0e61",
"Health is the final cause of walking about.": "9f280ff9326b",
"His most useful tool for explaining anything was a set of four questions, later called the four causes. In the Physics, in R. P. Hardie and R. K. Gayes translation, a cause can be that out of which a thing comes to be, such as the bronze of the statue; the form or the archetype; the primary source of the change, as the father is cause of the child; or the end or that for the sake of which a thing is done, as health is the cause of walking about. He applied the same patience to animals. Defending biology against those who found it beneath philosophy, he wrote in the Parts of Animals that we must not recoil with childish aversion from the examination of the humbler animals. Every realm of nature is marvellous.": "ef56e2dce2ae",
"Ibn Rushd was known in Europe as the Commentator.": "44d80d4f478e",
"In 343 BCE King Philip of Macedon invited him to tutor his thirteen-year-old son, Alexander. What Aristotle taught the boy who would conquer the Persian Empire is unknown, and the relationship cooled later. In 335 BCE, as Alexander set out for Asia, Aristotle returned to Athens and founded his own school at the Lyceum, a grove sacred to Apollo. His followers became known as Peripatetics, from the covered walkway, the peripatos, where they talked. The Lyceum collected books, specimens, and the constitutions of 158 Greek cities. When Alexander died in 323 BCE, anti-Macedonian feeling swept Athens, and Aristotle, suspected as a Macedonian sympathizer, left the city. He died the next year on the island of Euboea.": "4535201d5cd6",
"In one sense, then, that out of which a thing comes to be and which persists, is called cause, e.g. the bronze of the statue, the silver of the bowl.": "b8445028a9dc",
"Logic, physics, biology, psychology, ethics, politics, rhetoric, and poetics each received a treatise with its own method. The idea that different subjects need different kinds of inquiry is Aristotles.": "caaf2a855b04",
"Platos student, Alexanders tutor, and founder of the Lyceum. He wrote on logic, nature, animals, the soul, ethics, politics, rhetoric, and poetry.": "c21a475049c1",
"The Metaphysics opens, in W. D. Rosss translation, with a sentence that could stand over the door of any library: All men by nature desire to know. Aristotle tried to satisfy that desire in every direction, and he sorted what he found. Logic came first, as the tool for all the rest, and his writings on it were later collected as the Organon, the instrument. Then came the theoretical sciences, which study what cannot be otherwise: physics, the study of nature, mathematics, and first philosophy, which later editors called metaphysics. The practical sciences, ethics and politics, study how to act well. The productive sciences, such as rhetoric and poetics, study how to make things. The names of our university departments still follow much of this map.": "993846944bde",
"The Prior Analytics gave the first theory of valid inference, the syllogism. It remained the core of logic in Europe and the Islamic world for two thousand years. The lesson on the three-part inference compares it with Buddhist logic.": "ab362ae4fac8",
"The great Andalusian commentator on Aristotle, whose works shaped how medieval Europe read him. Later lessons on the House of Wisdom return to him.": "b96acdff5dc3",
"We therefore must not recoil with childish aversion from the examination of the humbler animals. Every realm of nature is marvellous.": "8a7dc2efad70",
"What survives of Aristotle is mostly lecture notes and treatises, edited by Andronicus of Rhodes some three centuries after his death. His polished dialogues, which Cicero praised, are lost. After the fall of Rome his works largely disappeared from the Latin West, but they were translated into Syriac and Arabic, studied in Baghdad and Córdoba, and commented on by Ibn Rushd, whom Europeans called simply the Commentator. When Latin translations reached the new universities in the twelfth and thirteenth centuries, Aristotle became so central that Thomas Aquinas referred to him as the Philosopher. His physics was overturned by Galileo and Newton, and his biology was surpassed. But logic, ethics, and the idea that knowledge should be organized into fields with their own methods still carry his mark.": "9e0d507d59a8",
"Where Parmenides trusted reason over the senses, Aristotle began with what is observed and what people commonly believe, and reasoned from there. His biology made careful observation part of philosophy.": "3882fa5eb281",
